The re

                    Begin your application with the word WORKBENCH and use at least 8 Apply Points.



This is not an entry-level AI role.



I am looking for an experienced AI video-generation specialist who can create photorealistic real-world footage for a professionally produced educational YouTube channel.



The scenes will normally show ordinary small-business environments: an owner working at a desk, handling invoices, using a laptop, checking paperwork or carrying out other simple physical actions.



These scenes must feel filmed—not obviously generated.



This requires more than entering a prompt and accepting the first result. You must understand reference-image continuity, believable human movement, physical interaction, camera position, imperfect natural framing and the small visual details that expose AI-generated footage.



THE ROLE



You will create the real-world visual assets that are later passed to a separate motion designer and video editor.



You will not be responsible for:



writing scripts;



d
 the story;



creating UI screens;



producing voiceovers;



adding captions or music;



assembling the finished YouTube video.



The editorial direction, timing and required shots will already be provided.



WHAT YOU WILL RECEIVE



Each production batch will include:



the relevant script sections;



a timed shot and choreography brief;



descriptions of the required action and camera behaviour;



approved character and environment references;



wardrobe, lighting, workspace and prop-continuity requirements;



required start and end frames where appropriate;



file-naming and delivery instructions;



examples of what must remain unchanged between connected shots.



YOUR RESPONSIBILITIES



Create photorealistic reference images for approved real-world scenes.



Generate short AI video clips from those references.



Maintain the same person, face, body, clothing, workspace, lighting and props across connected shots.



Follow specific camera, framing and physical-action instructions.



Use natural, slightly imperfect compositions instead of sterile, perfectly centred AI framing.



Check every result for visible generation problems before delivery.



Generate better alternatives when the first result is not usable.



Make light corrections or clean-ups where generation alone is insufficient.



Keep source files, reference images and approved outputs clearly organised.



Record the tools, prompts, settings, seeds or references needed to reproduce an approved result.



Flag technically unreliable shots early instead of repeatedly spending credits without approval.



Deliver clean footage without captions, voiceover, music or decorative effects unless specifically requested.



THE QUALITY STANDARD



The audience should not be distracted by obvious AI problems such as:



changing faces, body shape, clothing, rooms or props;



unnatural hands or fingers;



weak contact between hands and physical objects;



floating, bending or changing objects;



impossible reflections, shadows or lighting;



rubbery body or facial movement;



strange eye movement;



unnecessary slow motion;



artificial camera movement;



characters staring blankly into the camera;



overly polished advertising compositions;



shots that look like generic AI-generated B-roll.



Photorealism is not only about image resolution.



A technically sharp image can still look artificial because the camera is too perfectly positioned, the room is too clean, the action is too smooth or the person does not move like a real person.



You must be able to notice and correct those problems.



REQUIRED EXPERIENCE



You must already have practical experience with professional AI image and video generation.



Suitable tools may include:



Veo or Google Flow;



Kling;



Runway;



Higgsfield;



Midjourney;



Flux;



Nano Banana;



ComfyUI;



Photoshop;



equivalent professional image or video-generation tools.



I care more about the quality and controllability of your results than about one specific platform.



This role is not suitable if:



you are still learning the basics;



you mainly create avatars or AI presenters;



your portfolio consists primarily of UGC advertisements;



you create automated slideshows or image montages;



you rely on one prompt and accept the first output;



you cannot maintain continuity between connected shots;



you cannot recognise obvious AI artefacts in your own work;



you need constant supervision;



you cannot provide original examples of work you personally created.



PAY AND WORKLOAD



The starting rate is US$10 per hour.



The initial workload will normally be 10–12 hours during an active production week. Work will arrive in planned production batches, so some weeks may be lighter than others.



This is intended to become an ongoing working relationship. Hours and pay can increase as the channel and production system grow.



Any paid generation costs must be agreed before use. Do not purchase additional software or credits without approval.



SHORTLISTING AND PAID TEST



Applications will first be judged on portfolio quality and attention to the instructions in this advertisement.



Shortlisted applicants will then receive a small paid test using supplied reference images and a short real-world action similar to the actual work.



The test will assess:



visual realism;



character and environmental continuity;



physical interaction;



camera judgement;



ability to follow a precise brief;



file organisation;



communication;



whether you identify and correct AI problems yourself.



No unpaid custom sample will be requested.



HOW TO APPLY



Answer every question below:



How long have you been producing AI-generated images and video professionally?



Which AI image and video tools do you currently use?



Provide links to three examples you personally created.



Include at least one example of a realistic person physically interacting with an object.



Include at least one example that maintains the same person or environment across multiple connected shots.



Which example best shows your ability to create footage that does not immediately look AI-generated, and why?



Describe your normal workflow from receiving a reference image to delivering an approved video clip.



Describe one visible AI problem you noticed and corrected rather than accepting.



How do you maintain the same character, clothing, room and props across different generations?



What do you do when a requested physical action repeatedly fails?



What is your current weekly availability and time zone?



Do you accept the US$10 starting rate and variable weekly workload?



Confirm that the examples supplied are your own work.



Link directly to the relevant examples.



Do not send a large general portfolio and ask me to search through it. Applications without relevant AI-generated image or video work will not be reviewed.



After answering every question, finish your application with the word NATURAL.
